      children: "The Mahabharata is the longest epic poem in the world, and the Bhagavad Gita sits inside it like a jewel in a crown — in the middle of the Bhishma Parva, as two vast armies face each other on the plain of Kurukshetra. Dhritarashtra, the blind king, cannot see the field; his charioteer-like boon, the all-seeing Sanjaya, narrates every event to him. The first speaker is Dhritarashtra himself, and his first question — \"what did my sons and the sons of Pandu do?\" — becomes the frame of the entire Gita. The second speaker is Sanjaya, the reporter with divine sight. Then Duryodhana speaks, counting his heroes to his teacher Drona. Then Arjuna speaks — and for the last time in the epic, he speaks like a man in command. After that, the man who could shoot through the eye of a moving fish cannot even hold his bow."
